Q1. Deadlock Debugging:

Debugging Analysis:
The deadlock occurs because both processes acquire the shared resources in different orders.
P1 locks R1 first and then requests R2.
P2 locks R2 first and then requests R1.

If both processes execute simultaneously:
P1 holds R1 and waits for R2.
P2 holds R2 and waits for R1.
This creates a circular wait, causing a deadlock.

Optimized Code:
Process P1
lock(R1);
lock(R2);
Critical Section
unlock(R2);
unlock(R1);

Process P2
lock(R1);
lock(R2);
Critical Section
unlock(R2);
unlock(R1);

Optimization:
Maintain a fixed resource allocation order (R1 → R2) for all processes. This removes the circular wait condition and prevents deadlock.

Result:Deadlock is eliminated without changing the process logic.

Q2. CPU Utilization Problem:

Debugging Analysis

The scheduler repeatedly shows:
Ready → Waiting → Ready → Waiting

This indicates that processes are I/O-bound. They frequently wait for I/O operations, leaving the CPU idle even though processes exist in the system. As a result, CPU utilization remains at only 30%.

Optimization:
Use asynchronous (non-blocking) I/O so processes do not block unnecessarily.
Increase the degree of multiprogramming so another process can execute while one waits.
Use a scheduler such as Multilevel Feedback Queue (MLFQ) or optimize Round Robin parameters.

Result:
CPU idle time decreases and CPU utilization improves significantly.

Q3. Memory Bottleneck Debugging:

Debugging Analysis

Memory usage:

Code = 5 MB
Heap = 150 MB
Stack = 25 × 1 MB = 25 MB
Total = 180 MB

Although total memory is less than 2 GB, the application experiences frequent page faults because multiple threads access large memory regions, increasing memory pressure and reducing cache efficiency.

Optimization:

Optimization 1: Use a thread pool to reduce the number of active threads.

Optimization 2: Optimize heap allocation by using memory pools, releasing unused memory, and improving data locality.

Result:
Page faults decrease, memory performance improves, and execution becomes faster.

Q4. Starvation Debugging:

Debugging Analysis:

P5 belongs to Q3 (Batch Queue), which uses FCFS scheduling. Since higher-priority queues (Q1 and Q2) continuously receive new processes, P5 never gets CPU time.
This is a classic case of starvation.

Optimization:
Implement Aging, where the priority of a process increases the longer it waits.
Alternatively, use a Multilevel Feedback Queue (MLFQ) so long-waiting processes can move to higher-priority queues.

Result:
Every process eventually receives CPU time, ensuring fairness and preventing starvation.

Q5. Context-Switch Overhead Debugging:

Debugging Analysis:

Given:
Context switches = 20,000/sec
Average burst time = 2 ms
Time quantum = 1 ms
High I/O frequency

Since the time quantum (1 ms) is smaller than the average CPU burst (2 ms), most threads are preempted before completing. Frequent I/O waits also cause repeated scheduling, resulting in excessive context switching.

Optimization:
Optimization 1: Increase the time quantum to 4–5 ms so threads can complete more work before preemption.

Optimization 2: Reduce I/O wait time using asynchronous I/O, buffering, or batching of I/O requests.

Result:
The number of context switches decreases, CPU overhead is reduced, and overall system performance improves.
